WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.292
Defense in depth uses multiple layers so that failure of one control

00:00:05.292 --> 00:00:07.887
does not expose the entire operation.

00:00:07.887 --> 00:00:11.781
The layers include governance, people, physical security, architecture, identity,

00:00:11.781 --> 00:00:14.809
endpoint protection, monitoring, recovery, and incident response.

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:18.270
Their value comes from how they work together.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:22.597
Physical security protects control rooms, cabinets, network equipment, field devices,

00:00:22.597 --> 00:00:23.895
and maintenance interfaces.

00:00:23.895 --> 00:00:28.221
Network segmentation restricts unnecessary pathways and creates points for monitoring.

00:00:28.221 --> 00:00:33.412
Access control assigns individual identities and least privilege where systems support them,

00:00:33.412 --> 00:00:37.739
while managed gateways can protect devices with limited native capability.

00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:42.065
Remote access should use approved entry points, strong authentication, authorization,

00:00:42.065 --> 00:00:43.795
time limits, and monitoring.

00:00:43.795 --> 00:00:46.824
Connections should be disabled when not needed.

00:00:46.824 --> 00:00:51.150
Network and host monitoring can identify new devices, unexpected protocols,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.178
cross-boundary activity, administrative changes, and abnormal behavior,

00:00:54.178 --> 00:00:58.505
but collection must be paired with review and response ownership.

00:00:58.505 --> 00:01:02.398
Vulnerability management combines inventory, vendor advisories, exposure, process consequence,

00:01:02.398 --> 00:01:04.994
testing, patch planning, and compensating controls.

00:01:04.994 --> 00:01:08.888
Backups should cover controller logic, device configuration, applications, servers,

00:01:08.888 --> 00:01:09.753
and documentation.

00:01:09.753 --> 00:01:15.377
Restoration must be practiced, and recovery plans must account for physical process state.

00:01:15.377 --> 00:01:19.703
Incident response plans should include cybersecurity, operators, engineers, safety personnel,

00:01:19.703 --> 00:01:21.434
vendors, leadership, and communications.

00:01:21.434 --> 00:01:27.491
Response actions such as isolation can protect systems but also remove visibility or control.

00:01:27.491 --> 00:01:30.519
Teams need preplanned authority and safe options.

00:01:30.519 --> 00:01:33.980
Every control should be evaluated for operational effect.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:37.874
Test changes, schedule maintenance, define rollback, and monitor results.

00:01:37.874 --> 00:01:42.633
Defense in depth is not the number of security products deployed.

00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:45.228
It is the presence of independent,

00:01:45.228 --> 00:01:50.420
verified protections that reduce credible risk while supporting safe and reliable operations.
